 "figures": {
  "changes": {
   "title": "Each rule, with handover and without",
   "caption": "Each row is one other rule. First: what adding handover to a world of B2 with that rule changes (batch X), at iteration 1,000 \u2014 in orange, the share of the agents holding three tokens or fewer; in violet, the diversity score; a dot for each seed, 1 and 2. Then: what it changes in the marks of the health bar the world meets. Last: the share of a contested world the brains of 1,000 of the world with both rules hold after 100 iterations against those of 500, the mean of two contests, a dot for each seed; the dashed line marked B2 is the median of the lab's ten B2 worlds, played the same way.",
   "recipe": "**Runs.** Batch X's pilot worlds (B2 at 10,000 tokens, handover and one other rule, seeds 1 and 2, `GraphOfLifeRuns/.pilots/X/`) and their controls: the same rule alone, from the batch or lab experiment that made it.\n\n**Data.** From each world's checkpoint at iteration 1,000 (`research/atlas/at.py --at 1000`, `GraphOfLifeRuns/.pilots/atlas/at1000.json`) and the contests in `GraphOfLifeRuns/.pilots/batch_TUV_T100.json` and `rejudge_T100.json`.\n\n1. Read each world as `research/atlas/profile.py` reads every world.\n2. Subtract the world with the rule alone from the world with both, seed by seed.\n3. Seat the brains of the two eras on alternate agents of each era's world, play 100 iterations, and count the agents the later brains hold.\n\n**To make it again:** `python3 book_figures.py handovercombos`."
  }
